---
/**
 * FinalCta — THE ONE CALL-TO-ACTION CARD, for every route.
 *
 * ── WHY THIS IS NOW SHARED, 2026-08-03 ──────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Owner: "You must capture all the CTAs into cards." Measured on the built site
 * at :8095 before changing anything, across all 43 routes: 24 closing or
 * next-step CTA blocks, and exactly ONE of them sat on a card — this one. The
 * other 23 were a heading, a line of copy and a button row on the bare page
 * background, written FIVE different ways:
 *
 *   .fc            1 route   the card (this file)
 *   bare <Section> 13 routes  /about /changelog /cookie-preferences /crowmark x2
 *                             /crowmark-buyers /faq /integrations /resources
 *                             /roadmap /sources /tools /tools/ppn-002-calculator
 *   .cmp-cta        5 routes  a full-bleed band, own h2, own sub, own actions
 *   .sec-final      5 routes  a full-bleed band, own eyebrow, own h2, own links
 *   .gl-cta         2 routes  a full-bleed band, own h2, own sub
 *   .gx-cta         1 route   a full-bleed band, own h2, own sub, own links
 *
 * The four bands each restated Section.astro's head block — heading size,
 * measure, gutter, eyebrow — which specs/PLATFORM-CHARTER.md already names as
 * open debt ("five closing-CTA recipes"). Card-ifying each page separately would
 * have made that six. So the answer is the one the charter states: a shared
 * component beats a repeated pattern. This file became that component, the four
 * bands were deleted, and the thirteen bare Sections became call sites.
 *
 * WHAT A CALL SITE PASSES: eyebrow, title, standfirst, id — the same four props
 * Section.astro already takes, because this IS a Section with a card around it
 * and nothing about the head block is redeclared here. The default slot is the
 * action row, which this component wraps and centres so no page has to own a
 * `.row` rule. `slot="aside"` is anything under it: a free-tool row, a legal
 * line, a compare link.
 *
 * IT IS NOT ALWAYS LAST, so the closing gap is on `:last-child` rather than
 * unconditional. Two call sites are mid-page next-step blocks (/crowmark's free
 * calculator, /tools) and must not push a section below them down.
 *
 * ── DESIGN: THE LEGACY CTA, ported. Owner decision, 2026-08-02, recorded in
 * specs/HOMEPAGE-DECISION-TABLE.md: "Keep the legacy CTA from the live
 * homepage." Six Figma variants (C1-C6) were rendered and NONE was taken,
 * including the one I recommended. Nothing from C1-C6 may be carried across
 * here by halves. The source is `index.html` at the repo root, section 8,
 * `.nt-cta-banner`.
 *
 * WHAT WAS PORTED. The eyebrow "Ready when you are"; the heading "See it on your
 * own tender."; both actions and both hrefs; and the free-tool row with both of
 * its links. The card chrome: near-black two-stop wash, one hairline, pill
 * radius, and the three blurred screen-blended blobs that are what give the
 * production card its glow rather than leaving it a flat dark panel.
 *
 * THE BODY WAS SHORTENED, 2026-08-02, AND ITS TWO CLAIMS BOTH SURVIVED. The
 * legacy standfirst ran 25 words; the sitewide cut brought it to 16. What was
 * removed was "through the engine, end to end, on your own evidence", which is
 * scene-setting. What was NOT removed, and may not be, is either of the two
 * trust signals named below: "we will run it with you" and "Access is limited
 * and by request". A shorter sentence that dropped one of those would be a
 * different promise, not a tighter one.
 *
 * THE TRUST SIGNALS ARE THE COPY, and there are exactly two, both true.
 * "Access is limited and by request" and "Bring a live notice and we will run
 * it through the engine with you" are statements the company makes about its
 * own terms and its own service. They are unverifiable from outside, and that
 * is fine: a company may describe its own policy. What is NOT here, and must
 * never be added, is the other kind — customer names, logos, testimonials,
 * ratings, counts of users. There are none, so there are none on the page. Nor
 * is there a win-rate claim, here or anywhere: refusing to claim one is the
 * product's position, not an omission to be tidied up later.
 *
 * REQUEST ACCESS IS A WEB FORM AND NEVER A BARE mailto:. Standing rule. The
 * href carries `?enquiry=limited-access`, which is a WIRE VALUE read by
 * /contact to preselect the subject, and `#contact-form` to land on the form
 * rather than the top of that page. Neither is copy; do not "tidy" them.
 *
 * TWO DOORS, DELIBERATELY. A high-commitment one (request access, book a demo)
 * and a zero-commitment one (the free calculator and the methodology behind
 * it). The free row is the only no-account route off this page and it stays
 * even though it sends people out of the funnel, because a reader with no next
 * step does not convert later, they leave.
 *
 * THE HEAD BLOCK COMES FROM Section, NOT FROM HERE. Centred head blocks and the
 * gradient capsule eyebrow are owner decisions implemented once in
 * layout/Section.astro. The previous build of this file hand-rolled its own
 * eyebrow, heading and measure, which is how a design system dies quietly. The
 * card is a wrapper AROUND that primitive, so it decorates the head block
 * without redeclaring one property of it.
 *
 * TWO THINGS FROM THE LEGACY MARKUP ARE DELIBERATELY NOT PORTED:
 *   - `<span class="paid">` put a teal-to-violet gradient on the last three
 *     words of the heading. Section owns heading rendering and takes a plain
 *     string; reproducing the span would mean restating heading styles here,
 *     which is the exact duplication this component was rewritten to remove.
 *   - `.nt-reveal`, the entrance animation. Content that fades in is briefly
 *     unreadable by design and permanently unreadable when the observer does
 *     not fire, which has already shipped on this site twice.
 *
 * NO JAVASCRIPT. Every word, both buttons and both free links are in the HTML
 * and painted at first render.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';

interface Props {
  /** Passed straight to Section. Nothing about the head block is owned here. */
  eyebrow?: string;
  title?: string;
  standfirst?: string;
  id?: string;
  /**
   * The zero-commitment row under the buttons, as outlined pills.
   *
   * A PROP RATHER THAN A SLOT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS ROW IS HERE.
   * Astro gives slotted content the CALLING page's scope hash, so a component's
   * scoped styles cannot reach it — a slotted link row would have to be styled
   * at every call site, which is the duplication this component exists to
   * remove. Six routes shipped this row six ways (`.fc__free`, `.sec-final-links`,
   * `.gx-cta-links`, `.compare`, and two bare `<p>`s). One recipe, one place.
   */
  links?: { label: string; href: string; target?: string }[];
  /**
   * One muted line under everything.
   *
   * It was introduced to hold the company line four routes carried as a local
   * `.reg` or `.legal` rule. It never took that job on: the four paragraphs
   * stayed on their pages, and on 2026-08-04 they were deleted outright (O-17)
   * rather than moved, because each was the footer's statutory disclosure
   * stated a second time on the same page. The prop is no longer for company
   * information.
   *
   * IT HAS TWO CALLERS FROM 2026-08-07: /tools, and the homepage, which passes
   * the access terms here after they were moved off ProofGate. That is the job
   * this prop is actually for — a quiet line qualifying the ask, at the ask —
   * as distinct from the statutory disclosure it was mistakenly built to hold.
   */
  note?: string;
  /**
   * Extra classes on the card. Used for nothing today and present because the
   * alternative is a page reaching for `.fc` in its own stylesheet, which is a
   * class it does not own.
   */
  class?: string;
}

const {
  eyebrow,
  title,
  standfirst,
  id,
  links = [],
  note,
  class: className = '',
} = Astro.props;
---

<div class:list={['fc', 'surface', className]} data-spot="off" data-lit data-lit-threshold="0.4">
  {/*
    The mesh. Two blurred lights INSIDE the card, screen-blended, one violet
    high left and one achromatic low right. It was THREE, in three hues, until
    2026-08-05. This layer is still the whole difference between the production
    card and a flat dark rectangle, and it was lost once already when this
    section was ported as semantic markup and the decorative layer was dropped
    with it, so what changed is the palette and never the presence.

    THIS IS THE REFERENCE RIG, and the note is worth keeping in front of anyone
    editing it. Figma `149:98`: "the card's three screen-blended blobs are
    ALREADY SHIPPED and are the reference rig. Nothing above invented a lighting
    language; it generalised this one to the other five sections."

    WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY THE CUT LANDED HERE TOO RATHER THAN ONLY UPSTREAM. The
    five section fields generalised from this card were reduced to one hue each
    on the same day, under the owner's rule of 2026-08-05, and a reference rig
    left at three hues would have put the original out of step with every copy
    of it. The geometry, the blur, the blend mode and the scale are untouched.
  */}
  <div class="fc__mesh" data-field aria-hidden="true">
    <b class="fc__m1"></b>
    <i class="fc__m2"></i>
    {/* The cursor light and the grain. Inside the mesh, which is z-index 0
        under the z-index 1 content layer, so neither can put light on the
        surface a word sits on. <i> rather than <b>: a <b> child is the rig the
        shared ambient layer drifts and phases, and another would join it.
        `--m2` is an <i> for the same reason and to the same end, which is
        recorded beside its rule below. */}
    <i class="sv-spot"></i>
    <i class="sv-grain"></i>
  </div>

  {/*
    THE SPECULAR, AND IT IS ON THE SAFEST SURFACE ON THE PAGE. A 1px highlight
    travelling the card's TOP EDGE only. The top edge is not text, nothing sits
    on it, and nothing reads across it, so light there costs no contrast
    anywhere. Sweeping the card's FILL would put light under two buttons and a
    paragraph, which is the one thing the motion rules forbid outright.
  */}
  <span class="fc__spec" aria-hidden="true"></span>

  <div class="fc__in">
    <Section eyebrow={eyebrow} title={title} standfirst={standfirst} id={id}>
      {/* The action row is owned here, not at the call site. Thirteen pages each
          carried a `.row { display:flex; justify-content:center }` rule for
          exactly this; all thirteen are deleted. */}
      <div class="btn-row"><slot /></div>

      {
        links.length > 0 && (
          <p class="fc__free">
            {links.map((l) => (
              <a
                class="control"
                href={l.href}
                target={l.target}
                rel={l.target === '_blank' ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
              >
                {l.label}
              </a>
            ))}
          </p>
        )
      }

      <slot name="aside" />

      {note && <p class="fc__note">{note}</p>}
    </Section>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /*
   * THE CARD SITS ON THE MEASURE, LIKE THE LEGACY ONE. `width` plus a token
   * max-width rather than a bare max-width: at 390px a measure-width card with
   * `margin-inline: auto` runs to both viewport edges, and the closing section
   * of the homepage is the last place to ship an element bleeding off screen.
   * The gutter is subtracted here; Section then applies its own gutter inside,
   * so the copy is inset from the card edge by exactly one gutter at every
   * width. Nothing about the measure or the padding is redeclared.
   */
  .fc {
    position: relative;
    width: calc(100% - 2 * var(--gutter));
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    /*
     * NEITHER `border` NOR `border-radius` IS DECLARED HERE ANY MORE, AND
     * REMOVING THE BORDER IS A HOVER FIX RATHER THAN TIDYING.
     *
     * styles/surfaces.css sets both on `.surface` and its own instructions are
     * explicit: "Do not restate background, border, border-radius, box-shadow,
     * backdrop-filter or the hover in a component's own styles ... a card that
     * restates them is how the count got to twenty-one." Two of the three
     * declarations here were exactly that. The background below is the
     * deliberate exception and its argument follows underneath; the border and
     * the radius had no argument, they were a copy.
     *
     * AND THE COPY WAS LOAD-BEARING IN THE WRONG DIRECTION. `.surface:hover`
     * brightens `border-color` to --c-border-glow from inside @layer
     * components. Astro's scoped styles are UNLAYERED, and an unlayered
     * declaration beats a layered one whatever its specificity, so
     * `border: 1px solid var(--c-border)` written here pinned this card's edge
     * at its rest value. The closing card was the only card on the site whose
     * border did not answer the pointer.
     *
     * MEASURED, 1440, 2026-08-03, on the built page with the pseudo-class
     * forced through CDP so nothing depends on where a mouse lands:
     *
     *   without the restated border   0.18 -> rgba(45,212,191,0.38)   brightens
     *   with it re-injected verbatim  0.18 -> rgba(160,180,228,0.18)  DEAD
     *
     * The second reading was taken by re-injecting the exact declaration at the
     * same scope selector rather than by measuring an older build, because
     * astro/dist was being rebuilt by another agent while this work was in
     * progress and was therefore not a baseline. An earlier version of this
     * note recorded the opposite conclusion from that contaminated build; it
     * was wrong and this replaces it.
     */
    /* Clear of the section above, which ends on its own --sec-pad-y. */
    margin-block: 0;
    /*
     * THE CLOSING CARD IS THE FOURTH SURFACE STEP: A SURFACE UNDER A LIGHT.
     *
     * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md measured this section at 0.01 gradient
     * coverage against 0.32 to 0.64 elsewhere, and it is the section that has
     * to read as arrival. It was --c-panel, which is one step below the raised
     * value and half a step above the page — a card that was barely a card.
     *
     * --c-lit is the value for exactly this: three screen-blended blobs sit
     * inside it, so it IS a surface directly under a light and the token now
     * says so. Still a two-stop wash leaning violet at the far corner, because
     * a flat fill is the strongest PowerPoint tell there is; the wash simply
     * starts one step higher.
     */
    --ramp-angle: 135deg;
    --ramp-from: var(--c-lit);
    --ramp-to: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-raised) 78%, var(--c-ambient-deep));
      /* Written here, not var(--grad-ramp). A custom property's var()s are
         substituted where it is DECLARED, so a knob token on :root bakes in its
         fallbacks and inherits finished. Measured: --grad-ramp computes to
         `linear-gradient(180deg, #161B2F, #0E1220 100%)` on every element,
         whatever knobs it sets. It hid every blog hero photo behind an opaque
         rectangle before this was found. Colours still come from tokens. */
    background: linear-gradient(
      135deg,
      var(--c-lit),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-raised) 78%, var(--c-ambient-deep)) 100%
    );
    /* Keeps the blobs inside the radius. They are sized to overhang. */
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* THE CLOSING GAP, AND ONLY WHEN IT IS THE CLOSE. The card used to carry
     `margin-block: 0 var(--sec-pad-y)` unconditionally, which was correct while
     it existed on one route as the last thing on the page. It is now also a
     mid-page next-step block on /crowmark and /tools, where an unconditional
     bottom margin would push the section under it down by a full section pad
     for no reason. `:last-child` asks the question the margin was really about. */
  .fc:last-child { margin-block-end: var(--sec-pad-y); }

  .fc__mesh {
    position: absolute;
    /* Scroll-driven parallax throw. The closing card is short, so its field
       travels least. See styles/tokens.css. */
    --par: 18px;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Both lights, whichever tag carries them. Screen, not normal: these are
     lights on a dark panel, and normal blending would paint grey discs over
     it. */
  .fc__m1,
  .fc__m2 {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(72px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  /* THE ONE HUE ON THIS CARD, AND IT IS VIOLET NOW.
     The rig was teal high-left, violet low-right and a cyan bloom across the
     top centre: three lights in three hues on the block that carries the
     page's two most important controls. This card asks for something, so it is
     the last place peak saturation should be spent on atmosphere.
     `--m1` keeps its geometry and takes the site's ambient violet. */
  .fc__m1 {
    width: 420px;
    height: 420px;
    left: -6%;
    top: -44%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
  }

  /* THE FILL LIGHT, ACHROMATIC AND STILL.
     Geometry is the old violet field's, so the card keeps its two opposite
     corners and reads as lit rather than as washed from one side. The fill is
     the ink role at 50%. --c-text rather than --c-white because a component
     names a role; check-palette-roles.js rule 1 fails on the token.

     50% RATHER THAN THE 62% THAT MATCHES THE OLD FIELD'S LUMINANCE, for the
     reason recorded at length beside `.hero__amb--2` in HeroStack.astro: a
     grey lift has no hue for the eye to attribute it to, so an achromatic
     light matched on luminance reads as haze where the chromatic one read as
     light, and it has to be taken down to read as light again. The correction
     is smaller here than in the hero, and deliberately: this field sits on a
     raised panel rather than on near-black, so it already has a lit ground
     under it and less of the effect to fight.

     IT IS AN <i>, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT STILL. The shared ambient layer in
     styles/tokens.css drifts `[data-field] > b`, so the tag decides whether a
     field breathes. One light per section breathes and the fill light does
     not; the composition is identical and A-111's always-on count halves.
     The cyan `--m3` is deleted rather than neutralised: three lights on a
     420px-tall card was one more than the composition needs. */
  .fc__m2 {
    width: 400px;
    height: 400px;
    right: -6%;
    bottom: -48%;
    background: radial-gradient(
      circle,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 50%, transparent),
      transparent 68%
    );
  }

  /* Above the mesh. Without this the blobs paint over the copy. */
  .fc__in {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  /* ══ M6 · ARRIVAL, NOT ACTIVITY ═══════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Figma `149:2` — "The last section should feel like arrival, not activity".
   * TRIGGER sweep on view at threshold 0.4; the drift is always on.
   * CADENCE 3 beats of sweep; the drift is --m-drift.
   * PLAYS: the sweep ONCE, the drift a loop. Both looped until 2026-08-05.
   * REDUCED MOTION neither runs; the card is already lit.
   *
   * THE SWEEP USED TO LEAVE AND NOT COME BACK, THEN IT LOOPED, AND AS OF
   * 2026-08-05 IT LEAVES AND DOES NOT COME BACK AGAIN. The objection was and
   * remains the strongest one on this page: two buttons sit in this card, and a
   * highlight that keeps sweeping past a call to action reads as a loading
   * state. The looping version answered it with REST — a loading bar repeats
   * every one or two seconds, this crossed the card's top edge in 1.8s and left
   * the edge dark for 10.2s, and five passes a minute on a 1px line is a lit
   * object rather than a progress indicator. That was a good answer to a
   * question this section no longer has to be asked. The sweep is a one-shot
   * now, so the card is lit once as the reader arrives at it and is then simply
   * lit, which is what `149:2` asked for in the first place.
   *
   * `149:56` sets the speed deliberately: three beats across the measure is
   * half the hero light's speed, because speed is tone and this section is the
   * end of an argument rather than a demonstration of one. That relationship is
   * preserved — the hero is six beats over three planes, so it covers half a
   * plane per beat, and this covers a third of the card per beat.
   *
   * THE DRIFT IS NO LONGER DECLARED HERE. It is the shared ambient layer in
   * styles/tokens.css, reached by `data-field` on the mesh. This card's blobs
   * were the reference rig the other six sections were generalised from
   * (`149:98`), and the generalisation is now literal: the same rule drives
   * every drifting field on the page, which is six of them since the cut of
   * 2026-08-05 and was seventeen before it. The two things that changed in the
   * move are
   * the easing, from ease-in-out to --m-ease-breath, and the phase offsets,
   * which are negative rather than positive — the old `6s` and `12s` delays
   * meant blobs two and three sat perfectly still for the first six and twelve
   * seconds of the page's life.
   */
  .fc__spec {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .fc__spec {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      /* The card's own 1px top edge, inside its border radius. */
      top: 0;
      inset-inline: 0;
      height: 1px;
      z-index: 2;
      /* --grad-beam with a white bloom rather than a hue: the closing card's
         sweep is arrival, not a marker, so nothing on it names a colour.
         --beam-w is 8% because this element is the full card width. The bloom
         ran at 55% here against 70% in five other sections; the token holds
         70%, so this specular is fractionally brighter than it was. */
      /* --beam-hue REMOVED 2026-08-05: it never worked. A :root token bakes its
         var() fallbacks in at declaration, so this knob was inert and this beam
         painted teal while the comment below claimed a white bloom. --grad-beam
         is achromatic now, so the white this line asked for is what it paints. */
      --beam-w: 8%;
      background: var(--grad-beam);
      opacity: 0;
    }

    /* ONCE, ON REVEAL. It looped on --m-cycle until 2026-08-05, and the
       objection recorded above — that a highlight sweeping past two calls to
       action reads as a loading state — is now answered by the sweep not
       coming back rather than by the length of the gap between passes. That is
       a stronger answer than the one it replaces, and it is also A-111's:
       together with the twelve in Integrations.astro this was the last of the
       reveal-started loops that made the page's perpetual animation count
       climb as it was read and never come down.

       THREE BEATS, WHICH IS WHAT IT ALWAYS WAS. The window was 0% to 15% of a
       twenty-beat cycle, so the pass measured exactly three beats; the
       duration is those three beats now and the keyframe offsets below are the
       old ones multiplied by twenty thirds. `149:56` asked for three beats
       across the measure and still gets them. */
    [data-lit='on'] .fc__spec {
      animation: fc-spec calc(var(--m-beat) * 3) var(--m-ease-travel) 1;
    }

    /* NOTHING HERE TARGETS .fc__m1 OR .fc__m2, and that is the point
       of the change rather than a deletion. Astro's scoped styles are
       unlayered and would beat the shared rule, so a component that kept its
       own drift declaration would silently opt itself back out of the system.
       The mesh carries `data-field`; styles/tokens.css does the rest. */
  }

  /* THREE BEATS OF SWEEP, AND THE BLOCK IS NOW THOSE THREE BEATS AND NOTHING
     ELSE. The window was 0% to 15% of a twenty-beat cycle with the last
     seventeen twentieths of the block holding the light parked off the far
     edge; the duration is three beats now, so 2% of the cycle is 13.33% of the
     pass and 13% is 86.67%. Every fade lands at the same instant it did, on
     the same easing, because all four offsets kept their ratios.

     THE LAST OFFSET IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE RESTING STATE. The light crosses
     and leaves at zero opacity rather than resolving to where the element
     lives, which is what keeps this a figure rather than an entrance under
     check-motion.js rule 2. */
  @keyframes fc-spec {
    0%      { transform: translateX(-50%); opacity: 0; }
    13.33%  { opacity: 1; }
    86.67%  { opacity: 1; }
    100%    { transform: translateX(50%); opacity: 0; }
  }


  /*
   * THE FREE ROW, in the legacy treatment: outlined pills rather than plain
   * underlined links, so the zero-commitment door reads as a pair of choices
   * next to the two buttons instead of as body copy that happens to be blue.
   *
   * Legacy set these at 12.5px. They are --t-body here. The type scale was
   * lifted deliberately after the owner read the homepage as small and tight,
   * and re-importing a fixed 12.5px would put one row back under the old scale
   * on the page's most important control cluster.
   */
  .fc__free {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 10px;
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* The wash, the hairline, the ink and the hover are `.control` in
     styles/controls.css (A-52); the ring is the sitewide one in
     styles/motion.css, which the rule deleted from here restated value for
     value. What stays is the 44px target. */
  .fc__free a {
    /* 44px, WCAG 2.2 target size. Legacy shipped these at 6px padding on a
       12.5px line, which is roughly 26px tall and fails 2.5.8. */
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0 14px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /*
   * THE NOTE LINE. This was written for the company line that shipped on four
   * routes as a local `.reg` or `.legal` rule; those paragraphs were deleted on
   * 2026-08-04 (O-17) as duplicate statutory disclosure, so the recipe now
   * serves the one caller left, /tools.
   *
   * --t-micro and --c-text-muted, which is the same pair the footer's own legal
   * line uses. Nothing here is a new value.
   */
  .fc__note {
    margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /*
   * At 390px the blobs are wider than the card and the blur pushes the wash to
   * a near-flat violet, which loses the panel's edges rather than lighting it.
   * They are scaled with the card, not switched off: the glow is the design.
